    "problem": "Which organizations in Germany are not subject to the flexible remote work cover day rule?",
    "attempt": "In Germany, the implementation of remote work policies, including the \"flexible remote work cover day rule,\" can vary depending on specific laws and collective agreements. However, as of my last update, there isn't a widely recognized \"flexible remote work cover day rule\" that applies uniformly across all sectors in Germany. \n\nIf you're referring to regulations or guidelines related to remote work, certain organizations might be exempt based on their industry or specific circumstances. For example:\n\n1. **Public Sector**: Some public sector employees may have different rules due to collective agreements or specific legislation governing their employment.\n2. **Healthcare and Emergency Services**: Employees in healthcare and emergency services often have strict presence requirements due to the nature of their work.\n3. **Manufacturing and Construction**: Workers in these industries typically need to be physically present at the workplace due to the nature of their tasks.\n\nFor precise information, it's best to consult the relevant labor law statutes, collective bargaining agreements, or directly with the human resources department of the organization in question. Additionally, the German Federal Ministry of Labor and Social Affairs (Bundesministerium für Arbeit und Soziales) or the Federal Employment Agency (Bundesagentur für Arbeit) could provide more detailed guidance.",
    "solution": "1&1 Versatel GmbH, InternetX GmbH, STRATO AG, Sedo Holding GmbH, World4You Internet Services GmbH, home.pl S.A., subsidiaries, united-domains AG, we22 Aktiengesellschaft",
