ad penetration. I should look for evidence of a specific percentage of the workforce being trained. If the goal is a systemic change, the training must hit a critical mass.\n\nIs there a distinction between the \"topics\" and the \"mechanisms\"? The topic is diversity. The mechanism is the reduction of unconscious influence. If I find a program that only talks about different cultures (ethnicity, gender, etc.) without addressing the cognitive bias part, it fails the first half of the prompt. I must ensure the solution links the \"what\" (diversity topics) with the \"how\" (reducing unconscious influence).\n\nLet's re-evaluate the \"voluntary\" vs. \"mandatory\" aspect. If a program is voluntary, how does it reach 80% of a workforce? That is a massive hurdle. Usually, high-percentage coverage implies a requirement. But what if the program is designed to be a \"two-part diversity training course\" that is structured in a way that it is widely adopted? Or perhaps the \"voluntary\" nature refers to the specific participation in the deep-dive modules, while the overview is more standard? Actually, if the reference indicates it is a \"voluntary two-part diversity training course,\" I must reconcile how that addresses the culture. A voluntary deep-dive suggests that the company provides the tools for those who want to change, while the broad coverage might come from a different layer of the initiative.\n\nLet's look at the causal link. Program $\\rightarrow$ Awareness of unconscious thought processes $\\rightarrow$ Reduction of bias $\\rightarrow$ Inclusive culture. For this to work, the \"unconscious thought processes\" must be the central pedagogical unit. If the program is just \"Diversity 101,\" it is too shallow. It must explicitly tackle the cognitive science of bias. \n\nI am now considering if the \"inclusive work culture\" is an output of the training or a subject of the training. If it is a subject, the training teaches people how to be inclusive. If it is an output, the training is intended to create the culture. The prompt asks which programs \"address\" these things, so the program must contain elements of both.\n\nLet's test the strength of the \"two-part course\" idea. A two-part structure allows for: Part 1: Identification of diversity topics and unconscious mechanisms. Part 2: Application to workplace inclusion. This covers all bases: topics, bias mechanism, and culture. Does it fit the scale? If the data shows a high percentage of the workforce has completed it, then it is a successful systemic program.\n\nI must avoid the trap of thinking \"diversity training\" is a monolith. There is a difference between \"Diversity Awareness\" (knowing people are different) and \"Unconscious Bias Training\" (knowing why you treat them differently). The prompt specifically asks for the latter. Therefore, the most robust solution must highlight the specific focus on \"unconscious thought processes\" and the \"reduction of influence\" of these processes, rather than just general sensitivity training. I will focus my direction on the specific two-part structure and its focus on the cognitive aspect of bias to ensure it meets the depth required by the problem.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nDiversity training courses, overview of the topics of diversity, reduce the influence of unconscious bias, trained at least 80% of the workforce, unconscious thought processes, voluntary two-part diversity training course<|im_end|>"
