t if the standard is \"fair and inclusive recruitment,\" it sets the stage for a healthy social environment. Let's look at the mechanism. The mechanism of recruitment determines the composition of the workforce, which directly impacts the social fabric of the workplace.\n\nLet's try a different direction: what if the question is looking for specific regulatory compliance? That would mean the focus is on \"social standards.\" But \"social standards\" is a very broad term. It could mean anything from gender equality to preventing child labor in the supply chain. Since the question specifies \"employee well-being and workplace conditions,\" I should probably keep the focus on the internal workforce rather than the external supply chain, although in ESG, these are often linked. If I focus on the supply chain, I am straying from the \"employee\" part of the prompt. I must stay focused on the internal actors.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the list of potential domains. We have:\n1. Economic/Contractual: Remuneration, Working hours.\n2. Physical/Safety: Occupational health and safety.\n3. Social/Cultural: Social standards, Recruitment.\n4. Holistic/Support: Employee benefits, Working conditions.\n\nWait, \"working conditions\" is almost a synonym for the whole problem. If I say \"policies for working conditions include working conditions,\" I am being tautological. I need to see \"working conditions\" as the umbrella and the others as the components. Or perhaps \"working conditions\" is a specific category alongside \"social standards.\"\n\nLet's test the \"recruitment\" idea against a counterexample. If a company has great benefits but terrible, biased recruitment, is the workplace condition healthy? No. So recruitment is a necessary policy for maintaining the standard of the workplace. \n\nIs there a distinction between \"employee benefits\" and \"remuneration\"? Yes. Remuneration is the base salary and direct compensation. Benefits are the indirect supports like health insurance