-being.\nWorking conditions -> General environmental well-being.\n\nThis covers all bases. Now, I need to verify if I have any overlaps that are actually the same thing. Is \"working conditions\" just a summary of the others? In many contexts, yes. But if we treat it as a category of policy—meaning policies specifically governing the physical and social environment of the office—it stands alone.\n\nOne more check. Could \"social standards\" be the same as \"recruitment\"? No, recruitment is a process; social standards are the ruleset that governs that process and others. \n\nThe strongest path seems to be a multi-dimensional categorization that addresses the economic, physical, social, and procedural aspects of the employee experience. I have identified the specific domains: how they are paid and scheduled, how they are protected and supported, and how they are brought into and governed within the organization. I will focus on ensuring these domains are treated as distinct policy categories.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nemployee benefits, occupational health and safety, recruitment, remuneration, social standard, working conditions, working hours<|im_end|>"
