ory, but if WACKER offers them as part of a well-being strategy, they should be listed. However, maternity and paternity leave alone don't address the \"career flexibility\" part of the prompt fully; they are mostly about life events.\n\nWait, if an employee returns from a long leave, like parental leave, they might struggle to reintegrate into their previous role or tempo. This suggests a need for reintegration workshops. This link between a well-being event (parenthood) and a career mechanism (reintegration) creates a much stronger cohesive framework. This implies that the programs aren't just isolated benefits but a lifecycle of support.\n\nLet's test a different path. Could well-being be focused on mental health programs or wellness days? While plausible, the prompt asks for \"programs\" that promote both well-being and career flexibility. A wellness day doesn't necessarily offer career flexibility. If I focus only on health, I miss half the prompt. If I focus only on work hours, I miss the well-being aspect. The intersection seems to be around life-work integration. \n\nLet's re-evaluate the \"caregiving\" angle. Caregiving is a massive driver of stress and a barrier to career progression. If WACKER offers support for caregiving to family members or relatives, that directly addresses well-being (reducing stress) and flexibility (allowing the employee to manage duties). This isn't just about having time off; it's about having specific options to manage care. Does this include leave options? Yes, leave options would be the procedural mechanism to facilitate caregiving.\n\nI should avoid the hypothesis that \"flexibility\" just means \"working from home.\" While remote work is a common flexibility tool, it is often classified under \"work location\" rather than \"career flexibility.\" Career flexibility usually implies the ability to adjust the intensity or duration of one's career path. This brings me back to part-time models and sabbaticals. \n\nLet's scrutinize