company allows the employee to reshape their relationship with work to accommodate life's demands.\n\nI need to ensure I am not missing any nuances in \"caregiving.\" Does caregiving to family members encompass all relatives? If a program specifically mentions relatives, it is more comprehensive. The distinction between maternity/paternity leave (parent-focused) and caregiving to family/relatives (wider scope) is important for a complete answer. \n\nI will now pressure-test the connection between \"part-time models\" and \"career flexibility.\" Does a part-time model provide career flexibility? Yes, because it allows an employee to remain in their career trajectory while adjusting the work volume. Does it promote well-being? Yes, by allowing for better management of personal life. This seems to be the most consistent thread.\n\nThe strongest direction involves a dual-track approach: one track focusing on life-event transitions (maternity, paternity, caregiving for family/relatives) and another track focusing on structural work-life adjustments (part-time models, sabbaticals, reintegration workshops, and general leave options). This covers all components of the prompt: well-being (via stress reduction and life-stage support) and career flexibility (via temporal and structural adjustments). I must ensure the distinction between the types of caregiving is maintained to capture the full scope of the support offered.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\ncaregiving to family members, caregiving to relatives, family time, leave options, maternity/paternity leave, part-time models, reintegration workshops, sabbatical<|im_end|>"
