agement), 3. Technical and operational execution (IT, Procurement, Product). \n\nLet's stress-test the 'Accounting' vs 'Finance' distinction again. In the context of ESG, the convergence of financial and non-financial reporting is the most significant trend. Therefore, the function that handles the actual recording and verification of data—Accounting—is more precise for a governance committee than the broader 'Finance' department, which might be more focused on capital allocation and strategy.\n\nLet's reconsider the 'Product' function. In a digital company, the product *is* the business. Every decision in the product roadmap affects the scalability and sustainability of the service. This makes it a core pillar of governance, not an auxiliary one. Similarly, 'IT' is not just a support function; in a tech company, IT is the backbone of the environmental footprint.\n\nDoes this structure create a balanced set of mechanisms? We have the mechanisms of verification (Accounting, Legal), the mechanisms of implementation (Product, IT, Procurement, Facility Management), and the mechanisms of communication/alignment (HR, Investor Relations). This feels robust. I have moved from a vague 'corporate departments' idea to a specific map of how a digital entity manages its impact through different functional levers. The strongest direction lies in identifying these specific roles that bridge the gap between high-level ESG principles and the granular reality of a digital business model.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nAccounting, Facility Management, HR, IT, Investor Relations, Legal & Compliance, Procurement, Product<|im_end|>"
