e \"public arena\" affect them? Yes, through regulation and social license.\nDoes \"society\" affect them? Yes, through systemic risks and macro-trends.\nDo \"clients of brokers\" affect them? Yes, through market demand and reputational contagion.\nDoes the \"own workforce\" affect them? Yes, through operational execution and talent retention.\n\nIs there any reason to exclude \"partners\"? No, they are essential to the business model. \n\nI need to ensure I am not just listing people but identifying \"groups.\" \nGroup 1: The people the company employs (Own workforce).\nGroup 2: The people the company works with (Partners, Employees of those partners).\nGroup 3: The people who use the services (Corporate clients, Private clients, Clients of brokers).\nGroup 4: The broader environment (Public arena, Society).\n\nOne potential error is treating \"society\" and \"public arena\" as the same thing. In ESG, \"society\" often refers to the broader community and human rights aspects, while \"public arena\" can refer to the political, regulatory, and media landscape. Keeping them separate covers both the social impact and the regulatory/political influence.\n\nAnother potential error is the redundancy of \"employees\" and \"own workforce.\" If I have to choose a direction, I must consider if \"employees\" is a standalone group. If \"employees\" and \"own workforce\" are both listed, the most logical distinction is that \"employees\" refers to the people working within the broker/partner network, while \"own workforce\" is the internal MLP staff. This distinguishes between the company's direct control and its indirect influence/materiality through its partners.\n\nLet's double-check the client types. \"Private clients\" (individuals), \"Corporate clients\" (businesses), and \"Clients of brokers\" (those who access the service through a third party). This covers the entire spectrum of the service's reach.\n\nThe strongest direction for the solution is to categorize these stak