ht. \n\nLet's consider a counter-argument: are \"tax havens\" part of governance? Governance is usually the act of governing. A tax haven is an entity being governed (or failing to govern). However, in the context of international tax compliance, the classification of a territory as a \"tax haven\" via an official list is a core component of the compliance regime. \n\nI have identified several distinct categories:\n1. Regulatory bodies (actors): German Federal Ministry of Finance.\n2. Regulatory instruments (mechanisms): EU Black List, regulation.\n3. Jurisdictional classifications (subjects of governance): tax havens.\n4. Legal boundaries (parameters): scope of application.\n5. Administrative procedures (compliance actions): draft letter of application.\n\nI need to ensure I am not including \"Germany\" as a term unless it is part of a formal title. \"Germany\" by itself is just a location. \n\nLet's refine the \"draft letter of application.\" In a compliance context, the \"application\" is the mechanism by which an actor seeks to satisfy a regulation. It is a procedural term essential to the compliance lifecycle.\n\nI must avoid the trap of including general words like \"tax\" or \"finance\" unless they are part of a formal name. \"Tax regulations\" is the subject, but \"regulation\" is the governance term. \n\nOne final check on \"tax havens.\" If I exclude them, I miss the specific subject of the compliance tension described. If I include them, I am identifying the target of the regulatory framework. Given the context of ESG, where the focus is often on the avoidance of non-compliant jurisdictions, this is a central term for compliance.\n\nThe strongest direction involves grouping the terms into the structural hierarchy of a regulatory system: the authority (Ministry), the tool (List/Regulation), the boundary (Scope), the target (Havens), and the procedure (Letter). This covers all aspects of how governance is enacted and how compliance is verified.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nEU Black List, German Federal Ministry of Finance, Germany, draft letter of application, regulation, scope of application, tax havens<|im_end|>"
