vernance or a specific digital ethics section. I will set privacy aside for now to see if there's a more direct connection to the core Social indicators.\n\nLet's re-examine the product aspect. I have product safety. But what about the people involved in the development of products? If a company conducts research, particularly in healthcare or biotechnology, the ethics of those studies are paramount. Clinical studies involve human subjects. The right to informed consent and the ethical treatment of participants in research are fundamental human rights. If the directive is truly \"overarching\" and aligned with ESG, it would likely encompass the ethical standards of the company's core business activities. If the company is a life sciences firm, clinical studies are not just a business process; they are a human rights touchpoint. \n\nLet's test a potential error. I might be tempted to include \"environmental protection\" because it is part of ESG. However, environmental protection is the \"E\" in ESG. While there is an overlap where environmental degradation impacts human rights, the question asks specifically for human rights topics addressed by the directive. Including environmental topics would be a category error. I must stay strictly within the human rights domain.\n\nLet's refine the categories. I have labor rights (fair pay, collective bargaining, freedom of association, exclusion of child/forced labor), workplace safety (occupational health and safety), social equity (equal opportunity), and product-related rights (product safety, clinical studies). \n\nIs there a distinction between \"freedom of association\" and \"collective bargaining\"? Yes. Freedom of association is the right to join or form groups, while collective bargaining is the process of negotiating through those groups. Both are distinct human rights protections often cited in ESG frameworks. \n\nWhat about the tension between different topics? For instance, does focusing heavily on product safety