hat, I might miss the fact that \"Environmental Management\" is listed under \"Business Environment.\" This means the company views environmental stewardship as a component of a supplier's overall business health and stability. This is a crucial distinction. A supplier with no environmental management is considered a \"risk\" to the business environment. Similarly, \"Social aspects\" like CSR are seen as part of the supplier's \"Sustainability.\"\n\nIs there any other way to group these? What if the criteria are actually: \n1. Economic (Price, Stability, Performance)\n2. Technical (Quality, Support)\n3. ESG (Environmental management, Packaging, CSR, Social competence)\nThis would be a cleaner division. However, the problem mentions \"Sustainability of the supplier\" as a primary heading which then includes \"business environment\" and \"social aspects.\" This implies that for Frequentis, sustainability is a broad concept covering both the environmental and the organizational/social health of the partner.\n\nLet's stress-test the \"Social aspects\" and \"Packaging\" distinction. If packaging is under \"Sustainability,\" and CSR is under \"Social aspects,\" then \"Sustainability\" is the umbrella term for the \"S\" and \"E\" of ESG, while \"Business Environment\" might cover the \"G\" (governance/stability). \n\nWait, I must be careful not to over-categorize. Let's look at the components provided:\nPrice (development, comparison)\nQuality (product, complexity, assurance)\nSupport (commercial, personal, technical)\nSustainability (business environment [stability, performance, flexibility, environmental management], packaging [environment-friendly], social aspects [CSR, social competence]).\n\nIf I am asked for the criteria used in ESG-related areas, I must decide if I should list all of them or only the ones that fit the ESG definition. If I list \"Price,\" a critic would say \"Price is not ESG.\" But if the company's evaluation process *is* the mechanism, and the question asks for the criteria used *in* those areas, I must identify which of these specific metrics are the ESG ones. The most direct ESG metrics here are: environmental management, environment-friendly packaging, CSR, and social competence. But \"stability\" and \"flexibility\" are part of the \"business environment\" under the \"Sustainability\" heading. \n\nLet's try a different angle. Maybe the \"ESG-related areas\" refers to the segments of the evaluation itself. If the company evaluates suppliers on four main pillars (Price, Quality, Support, Sustainability), then the ESG-related components are concentrated in the \"Sustainability\" pillar. However, \"environmental management\" is tucked inside \"business environment.\" \n\nIf I focus purely on the \"Sustainability\" pillar, I capture: \n- Environmental Management (part of business environment)\n- Business stability/performance/flexibility (as part of the sustainable business model)\n- Environment-friendly packaging\n- Social aspects (CSR, social competence)\n\nDoes \"Quality\" involve ESG? The mention of \"quality assurance systems\" could include environmental audits, but the text doesn't explicitly link them. Does \"Price\" involve ESG? Not explicitly. \n\nThe strongest direction for the solution is to identify the broad pillars used in evaluation and then specify how the ESG-specific metrics are nested within them, particularly under the \"Sustainability\" umbrella. The key is to recognize that \"Sustainability\" at this company is a composite criterion that includes environmental management, social responsibility (CSR), and the stability of the business environment. I must ensure I don't exclude \"environmental management\" just because it's labeled as part of the \"business environment.\" The link is that a sustainable supplier is one that manages its environment, its social responsibilities, and its own business stability. \n\nI will double-check the grouping. \nPillar: Price -> Metric: Price development/