effect? If the project planning is flawed, it might lead to unrealistic procurement timelines, causing suppliers to drop out. Or, if the intended expenditure is too low, high-quality, sustainable suppliers might refuse to bid. This suggests the core issue might be centered around the pre-project phase where the budget and the technical requirements are set. If the intended expenditure does not account for the reality of the current market, the project is doomed to face higher construction costs and resource inefficiency from the start.\n\nWait, I need to differentiate between the cost and the efficiency. High construction costs are a symptom. The underlying challenge is why those costs are rising. If the costs rise because of changes in planning or changes in the construction manual, then the project is no longer following its optimized resource path. This creates a gap between the intended expenditure and the actual output. This gap is where sustainability is lost; when money is diverted to cover cost overruns caused by poor planning, there is less capital available for the green features or efficient technologies originally intended.\n\nLet's look at the pre-project phase more closely. If the challenges are concentrated here, they act as a domino effect. A failure in the pre-project phase—such as inadequate risk assessment regarding supplier availability or inaccurate cost estimation—leads to changes in planning during the actual construction. These changes require updates to the construction manual. These updates, in turn, drive up construction costs and deviate from the intended expenditure. This deviation is the mechanism that undermines resource efficiency. If the budget is consumed by reacting to these changes, the project fails to achieve its sustainability targets. \n\nIs it possible that the challenge is simply the physical construction process? For instance, material waste on site. While that is a resource efficiency issue, it doesn't explain the link to 