Let's look at the \"Report on opportunities and risks\" again. Can a company hide bad ESG performance here? Yes, by framing risks as external rather than internal. Therefore, this section is a critical tool for an analyst to cross-reference the non-financial statement. If the non-financial statement says \"we are green\" but the risk report says \"carbon taxes are a massive threat to our current model,\" there is a gap. This confirms that both are necessary for true insight.\n\nLet's synthesize the paths. \nPath 1: Focus on the \"ESG-labeled\" sections (Non-financial, Governance). \nPath 2: Focus on the \"Strategic\" sections (Strategy, Business Model, Management). \nPath 3: Focus on the \"Contextual\" sections (Economic Environment, Risks, Course of Business).\n\nTo provide a complete answer, I cannot choose just one path. A comprehensive insight requires the integration of all three. The strategy (Path 2) sets the intent, the non-financial and governance statements (Path 1) report the metrics and rules, and the risks and economic environment (Path 3) provide the reality check and the external drivers.\n\nI need to ensure I am not missing the \"Overview of the course of business.\" This section summarizes the year. If the company had a major ESG-related event, like a data breach or a major sustainability milestone, it would be summarized here. It acts as a bridge between the strategic intent and the non-financial results.\n\nLet's test the strength of including \"Business model and organisational structure.\" Some might argue this is just about how they make money. But in modern ESG, the \"S\" (Social) and \"G\" (Governance) are deeply tied to how a digital company is structured and how it interacts with its ecosystem. If the structure is decentralized, governance is harder; if the model is data-heavy, social risk is higher. This section is foundational.\n\nFinal check of the components: \nStrategic/Structural: Business model, Organizational structure, Corporate st