 perhaps strictly on environmental technical skills? If the Academy only focuses on green engineering or carbon accounting, it ignores the \"Social\" and \"Governance\" aspects of ESG. Such a narrow focus would fail to strengthen \"employee engagement\" across the company, as most employees are not engineers. Therefore, a purely technical environmental focus is likely incorrect. The solution must be broader.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the connection between communication and ESG. In the \"Governance\" pillar, transparency is king. If the Academy focuses on training staff in regular and transparent communication, it is building the governance capacity of the organization. This is a subtle but strong link. \n\nNow, I have a conflict. If I list \"sales\" and \"project management,\" am I saying the Academy is a department-specific training center, or is it using these roles as vehicles for ESG? If the Academy focuses on \"onboarding,\" it is a temporal focus. If it focuses on \"new managers,\" it is a demographic focus. To be a comprehensive set of \"focus areas,\" the list should likely encompass different dimensions of the employee experience.\n\nLet's check the strength of the \"Health Promotion\" idea. If health programs are part of the Academy, it moves the Academy from a purely cognitive learning center to a holistic development center. This is a significant shift. If I combine this with \"onboarding\" and \"new managers,\" I am covering the beginning of the employee journey and the middle-management layer. If I add \"sales\" and \"project management,\" I am covering the core operational functions. \n\nIs there a risk of over-extending? If I include too many things, it's no longer a \"focus.\" But if I include too few, it doesn't address \"ESG-related engagement.\" Let's look at the tension between \"regular and transparent communication\" and \"engagement.\" You cannot have engagement without communication. If the Academy's 2024 goal is to build a culture of engagement