uires that the workforce is not stagnant. Investing in human capital via training is a core social responsibility of a corporation to ensure the long-term employability of its staff. This is a very strong candidate.\n\nLet's refine the categorization. I have:\n1. Material/Legal foundations: Employment conditions, Working conditions.\n2. Human Capital development: Further training and development opportunities.\n3. Social/Relational dynamics: Cooperation, Communication and information, Leadership behaviour.\n4. Purpose/Role: Activity.\n\nI need to see if any of these are redundant or if they belong to a different ESG pillar. Is 'Communication' part of 'G' (Governance)? In a broad sense, yes, but in an employee survey, it is a social metric because it concerns the flow of human intelligence and the reduction of information asymmetry between power structures and workers.\n\nLet's test the 'Activity' category again. If I exclude it, am I missing a piece of the social puzzle? Social sustainability includes the 'decent work' agenda from the UN Sustainable Development Goals. Decent work involves not just pay, but the essence of the work itself. If the survey tracks satisfaction with the 'Activity' itself, it is measuring the fulfillment of the human element of labor.\n\nI have identified a comprehensive set of categories. I need to ensure I haven't included anything that is purely profit-driven or purely operational without a social component. For example, 'Sales performance' would be out. 'Profitability' would be out. 'Customer satisfaction' would be out. The categories I am left with all focus on the employee's interface with the organization's social and structural systems.\n\nThe strongest direction for the solution involves grouping these categories into a cohesive model of social sustainability: the material/legal basis (Employment and Working conditions), the developmental basis (Training), and the relational/structural basis (Leadership, Cooperation, Communication,