ioritized' topics. Is there a distinction between regular compliance training and ESG-aligned training? If regular compliance covers fraud and antitrust, maybe the ESG-aligned training specifically adds the 'S' and 'G' layers like human rights and whistleblower protection. \n\nLet's test the hypothesis that the list is actually a comprehensive set of all topics that touch on ethics, rights, and transparency. If I include everything from anti-corruption to data protection, does it form a coherent ESG-aligned training suite? Let's look at the links. Anti-corruption and fraud link to G. Antitrust and conflicts of interest link to G. Human rights and anti-discrimination link to S. Whistleblower protection links to both S and G. Cybersecurity and data protection link to S (privacy as a right) and G (operational resilience). \n\nIs there a risk of over-inclusion? If I include 'environmental compliance' like emissions reporting, am I straying? The prompt asks for compliance topics. While environmental laws exist, 'environmental compliance' is often treated as a separate operational pillar in ESG, whereas 'compliance training' usually refers to the code of conduct. Let's refine the scope to the Code of Conduct style topics.\n\nLet's test a specific subset: anti-corruption, anti-discrimination, anti-money laundering, antitrust law, conflicts of interest, cybersecurity, data protection, fraud, human rights, whistleblower protection. Does this set make sense? It covers the legal requirements for fair business (anti-corruption, antitrust, AML, fraud, conflicts of interest), the ethical requirements for people (human rights, anti-discrimination, whistleblower protection), and the modern requirement for information integrity (cybersecurity, data protection). \n\nLet's try to find a reason to reject any of these. Why would 'cybersecurity' be in an ESG training? Because data breaches are a significant social and governance risk. Why 'whistleblower protection'? Because it is the mec