\"International\" section. If the text says \"Our sites in Germany (Berlin, etc.) and our sites in France (Paris, Lyon),\" I must strictly truncate the list at the end of the German section. The distinction between the actor (the company), the mechanism (the audit), and the output (the list of locations) is clear. The objective is the identification of the specific parameters of the audit's geographical coverage. \n\nThe strongest direction is to locate the specific \"Scope\" or \"Audit Object\" section of the document, extract all entities explicitly linked to the \"Germany\" or \"German\" designation within that scope, and verify each entity's city-level location to ensure a complete and non-redundant list. This prevents the error of including non-audited sites or non-German sites.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nBerlin, Braunschweig, Dresden, Düsseldorf, German sites, Leipzig, Würzburg<|im_end|>"
