l layers (IT, operations, sales, and contracts) that must change their workflows to meet the ESG goals.\n\nI should pause and consider a counterexample. If I claim that \"employees\" are the units, I would be wrong because an employee is an individual, whereas a unit is a collective functional group. If I claim \"the ESG committee\" is the unit, but it isn't mentioned, I am inventing facts. I must stick to what is explicitly or implicitly provided. \n\nIs there a distinction between the \"organisations\" and the \"units\" that I am blurring? Organizations are the entities: Commerzbank Aktiengesellschaft, mBank, and Commerz Real. Units are the internal divisions: IT, operations, sales, and contracts. This creates a two-tiered structure of implementation: the institutional level and the functional level. \n\nLet me challenge this. Are \"sales and contracts\" two separate units or one unit called \"sales and contracts\"? Usually, in corporate structures, sales and contracts might be part of the same commercial division, but they represent different procedural stages. I will treat them as distinct functional areas to be safe. \n\nI must also consider if \"operations\" is an organization. It is too vague. An organization requires a proper name. Operations is a function. Therefore, \"operations\" must be a unit. \n\nLet's refine the logic. The actors are the corporate entities (Commerzbank Aktiengesellschaft, mBank, Commerz Real) and the mechanisms for implementation are the functional units (IT, operations, sales and contracts). I need to ensure I don't miss any connection. If the context states that the ESG initiatives are being implemented across the group, then the involvement is dual-layered. \n\nOne more check: could \"Commerzbank Aktiengesellschaft\" be the only organization, and the others are just related terms? No, mBank is a distinct, well-known financial institution. Commerz Real is also a distinct entity. They must all be included as organizations. The units 