 of how the Taxonomy is applied. Many activities are primarily assessed under the lens of climate change mitigation or adaptation. For an activity to be considered \"climate-aligned\" under the Taxonomy, it must satisfy the DNSH criteria for all other objectives, including pollution prevention. This means that for a vast number of activities, the DNSH criteria for pollution are actually embedded within the technical screening criteria found in the Climate Delegated Act.\n\nThis realization shifts my focus toward the Climate Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2139. I need to determine where within this act the pollution criteria are located. If I look at the structure of the technical screening criteria, they are organized by activity. Within each activity, there are specific requirements to ensure no significant harm is done to other objectives. I must investigate if there is a specific section or appendix dedicated to these DNSH requirements. A search through the technical structure suggests that the criteria are detailed in Annex 1, and specifically, the requirements for DNSH across different objectives are often organized into appendices. I need to confirm if Appendix C of Annex 1 is the correct location for the pollution-related DNSH criteria.\n\nHowever, there is a temporal constraint. Legislation in the EU is not static. The Climate Delegated Act was subject to recent changes to refine these very criteria. If I only cite the 2021 act, my answer would be outdated and legally incomplete. I must account for the amendment, which is the Amended Climate Delegated Act (EU) 2023/2485. This amendment is essential because the \"definition\" of the criteria is tied to the most current version of the law.\n\nNow I must address a potential complication. Are the criteria strictly technical numbers or thresholds defined within the Delegated Act itself? When examining the substance of the pollution prevention objective, the text often does not provide a list of chemical concentrations or emission limits. Instead, it frequently mandates compliance with existing broader EU laws. For instance, it might state that an activity must comply with the requirements of current European legislation on chemicals. This means that the \"criteria\" are not self-contained within the Taxonomy Delegated Acts; they are functionally defined by a combination of the Taxonomy's procedural requirements and the substantive rules found in the wider body of European chemical and pollution laws.\n\nSo, I have three distinct components to integrate: the procedural vehicle which is the Climate Delegated Act, the specific location within that act which is Appendix C of Annex 1, the update which is the 2023 amendment, and the substantive source which is the existing European chemical legislation. I must ensure I do not confuse the objective itself with the criteria. The objective is \"pollution prevention and control,\" but the criteria are the rules that ensure this objective is met. The most robust solution must link the specific technical annexes of the amended climate-focused legislation with the external regulatory frameworks for chemicals that the Taxonomy explicitly relies upon to define what \"prevention and control\" actually means in practice. I will now stress-test this by verifying if the environmental delegated acts offer a conflicting definition, but since the DNSH requirements for climate-aligned activities are explicitly housed in the climate act's technical screening annexes, the path involving the amended Climate Delegated Act and the referenced chemical legislation remains the most accurate and comprehensive direction.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nAmended Climate Delegated Act (EU) 2023/2485, Appendix C of Annex 1, Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2139, current European legislation on chemicals<|im_end|>"
