riables. I must avoid the mistake of simply listing every abbreviation in the section. If I find EU or SDG, I must reject them. While they are abbreviations, they are context-setters, not categories for environmental or eligibility criteria. They describe the regulatory environment or the broader framework, but they do not categorize the specific criteria mentioned in the problem. I am also questioning the structure of N/EL. It is not a standard acronym like CCM; it is a notation for a dual state. I must ensure that the solution treats it as a definition of eligibility status rather than a separate environmental term. Let's test the hypothesis that the question asks for a single list. If I combine them, I might lose the distinction required by the prompt. The prompt asks for abbreviations to categorize environmental and eligibility criteria. This implies two distinct sets or a way to sort them. I will proceed by isolating the substantive environmental categories—the what of the criteria—and the procedural eligibility statuses—the whether of the criteria. I will validate each candidate by ensuring its definition maps directly to one of these two roles: describing an environmental objective or describing a status of eligibility. This bifurcated approach prevents me from mixing up the objective mechanisms with the procedural outcomes.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nCCM = climate change mitigation, CE = transition to a circular economy, EL = eligible, N/EL = non-eligible<|im_end|>"
