    "attempt": "Vienna International Airport (VIE) operates under a robust framework of regulatory and compliance requirements, including those related to procurement and sustainability as outlined in the EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities. While I don't have access to the most up-to-date internal procedures of Vienna Airport, I can provide an overview of common practices that airports and large organizations typically use to ensure supplier compliance with procurement regulations and EU Taxonomy governance standards.\n\n### Procurement Regulations Compliance\n\n1. **Supplier Selection Criteria**: Vienna Airport likely uses a set of criteria during the supplier selection process that includes compliance with procurement regulations. This may involve evaluating suppliers based on their adherence to legal and ethical standards, financial stability, and past performance.\n\n2. **Contractual Obligations**: Contracts with suppliers often include clauses that require compliance with specific regulations and standards. These contracts might also include provisions for audits and penalties for non-compliance.\n\n3. **Regular Audits and Inspections**: Regular audits and inspections are conducted to verify that suppliers are adhering to the agreed-upon standards and regulations. This could include both internal audits and third-party assessments.\n\n4. **Training and Awareness Programs**: Vienna Airport may offer training programs for its suppliers to ensure they understand the relevant regulations and how to comply with them effectively.\n\n5. **Reporting Mechanisms**: There are likely mechanisms in place for suppliers to report any issues or concerns regarding compliance, which helps in maintaining transparency and accountability.\n\n6. **Continuous Improvement Processes**: Ongoing review and improvement of procurement policies and procedures help ensure that they remain effective and up-to-date with changing regulations.\n\n### EU Taxonomy Governance Standards\n\nThe EU Taxonomy is a classification system designed to help companies determine whether their economic activities contribute to environmental sustainability. To ensure compliance with these standards, Vienna Airport might implement the following processes:\n\n1. **Environmental Impact Assessments**: Suppliers are required to conduct environmental impact assessments to demonstrate how their activities align with the EU Taxonomy's criteria for climate change mitigation and adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\n2. **Sustainability Reporting**: Suppliers are expected to provide detailed reports on their sustainability efforts and impacts, which are then reviewed against the EU Taxonomy criteria.\n\n3. **Due Diligence Requirements**: As part of the procurement process, due diligence is performed to assess the environmental and social impacts of suppliers' operations. This includes evaluating their supply chains to ensure alignment with the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Performance Metrics**: Specific performance metrics related to sustainability are established and monitored to track progress and compliance with the EU Taxonomy over time.\n\n5. **Stakeholder Engagement**: Engaging with stakeholders, including environmental groups and local communities, to gather feedback and insights on suppliers' sustainability practices can help ensure that these practices meet broader societal expectations and regulatory requirements.\n\nBy implementing these processes, Vienna Airport aims to maintain high standards of compliance with both procurement regulations and the EU Taxonomy, ensuring that all business activities contribute positively to environmental sustainability while also meeting legal obligations.",
