    "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system that defines which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It aims to provide companies and investors with a common language to assess whether an economic activity is truly contributing to environmental sustainability. The taxonomy covers six specific environmental objectives:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote the sustainable use and protection of water resources and marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support the transition towards a circular economy, including waste prevention, re-use, recycling, and other forms of recovery.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that prevent or reduce pollution in air, water, and soil.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that protect and restore biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nEach of these objectives has detailed criteria and thresholds that must be met for an activity to be classified as sustainable under the EU Taxonomy. These criteria include significant contributions to the environmental objective without causing significant harm to any of the other objectives, and compliance with minimum safeguards related to human rights and good governance.",
