ulation has chosen to regulate. But being eligible is not the same as being environmentally sustainable according to the technical standards. An activity could be in the scope of the regulation but fail to meet the technical screening criteria or the do no significant harm principles. For those that do meet all the requirements, the regulation uses a different term. It is not just about being in the scope; it is about being in compliance with the criteria. This suggests the concept of alignment. If an activity satisfies all the thresholds, it is considered aligned. I should consider whether these terms are applied to the activities themselves or to the financial metrics of a company. The regulation requires companies to disclose the proportion of their turnover, capital expenditure, and operational expenditure that meets the criteria. This means the classification terms must be able to describe the status of these economic values. Is there a distinction between taxonomy-aligned turnover and taxonomy-aligned capital expenditure? Yes, because they represent different economic flows. I must also be careful not to conflate the status of the activity with the status of the expenditure. An activity can be taxonomy-eligible, and a company can have taxonomy-eligible capital expenditures, but that does not automatically mean those expenditures result in taxonomy-aligned activities. The classification system appears to rely on a two-step distinction: first, whether an activity falls within the regulatory scope, which involves the concept of eligibility, and second, whether it satisfies the rigorous technical standards, which involves the concept of alignment. These two concepts then apply to different economic indicators like turnover, capital expenditure, and operational expenditure. The strongest solution must therefore distinguish between the status of being in-scope versus being compliant and demonstrate how these concepts are applied to different types of economic value.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\ntaxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-aligned economic activities, taxonomy-eligible capital expenditures<|im_end|>"
