  {
    "id": "test:000787:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
    "problem": "Which regulatory references are foundational to the calculation of OpEx KPIs under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
    "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for investors, companies, and policymakers to assess whether an economic activity is environmentally sustainable. When it comes to calculating Operational Expenditure (OpEx) Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) under the EU Taxonomy framework, several regulatory references are foundational:\n\n1. **Regulation (EU) 2020/852**: This regulation, also known as the Taxonomy Regulation, sets out the criteria for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It establishes the concept of \"do no significant harm\" (DNSH) and requires that activities not only contribute positively to one or more environmental objectives but also do not cause significant harm to any of the other six environmental objectives.\n\n2. **Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178**: This regulation supplements the Taxonomy Regulation by providing detailed technical screening criteria for specific economic activities. These criteria include thresholds and methodologies for assessing whether an activity contributes substantially to one or more of the six environmental objectives and does not cause significant harm to the others. The OpEx KPIs would be part of these detailed assessments.\n\n3. **Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/1249**: This regulation further refines the technical screening criteria, including updates and clarifications based on feedback from stakeholders. It provides more precise guidance on how to calculate and report OpEx in the context of the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD)**: Although not directly related to the EU Taxonomy, the NFRD (Directive 2014/95/EU) requires large public-interest entities to disclose non-financial and diversity information, including environmental data. This directive indirectly supports the implementation of the EU Taxonomy by promoting transparency and accountability in reporting.\n\n5. **Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR)**: SFDR (Regulation (EU) 2019/2088) requires financial market participants and financial advisers to disclose information about the integration of sustainability risks and the consideration of adverse impacts of investment decisions on sustainability factors. While SFDR is broader than just the EU Taxonomy, it complements the Taxonomy by requiring disclosures that may include OpEx KPIs relevant to sustainability.\n\nThese regulations collectively form the basis for the calculation and reporting of OpEx KPIs under the EU Taxonomy framework. Companies must ensure their OpEx calculations align with the detailed technical screening criteria provided in the Commission Delegated Regulations to accurately reflect their contribution to environmental sustainability and compliance with the \"do no significant harm\" principle.",
    "solution": "Annex I Point 1.1.3.1, Annex II, Del. Reg. C&P, Taxonomy Regulation Article 8, subsection 1.1.3.2 of Annex I",
    "question_type": "factoid",
    "category": "EU Taxonomy",
    "reasoning_trace": "The objective is to pinpoint the exact regulatory anchors used to compute Operating Expenditure (OpEx) Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) within the EU Taxonomy framework. A correct solution must distinguish between the high-level mandate for disclosure and the granular technical criteria that allow a practitioner to classify a specific expenditure as aligned. Ambiguity arises in the term foundational; does it refer to the primary Regulation that establishes the existence of the KPIs, or the Delegated Acts that provide the mathematical and qualitative thresholds for those KPIs? To solve this, I must distinguish between the primary actor, the European Commission, the mechanism of disclosure, and the technical outputs required for reporting.\n\nInitially, one might look at the Taxonomy Regulation (EU) 2020/852 itself. Article 8 of this regulation is the core mechanism for non-financial undertaking disclosure. It mandates that companies report their alignment regarding turnover, CapEx, and OpEx. However, Article 8 is a procedural rule—it tells you that you must report, but it does not provide the technical screening criteria required to determine if a specific Euro spent on maintenance, repairs, or administration counts as taxonomy-aligned. Therefore, citing only Article 8 is an incomplete path because it addresses the obligation of the actor rather than the specific calculation methodology needed for the output.\n\nI should shift focus to the Delegated Acts, specifically those defining the technical screening criteria (TSC). The calculation of OpEx KPIs is intrinsically tied to whether an activity meets the TSC. The framework is not just the parent Regulation but the specific Commission Delegated Regulations. These regulations contain the actual definitions of what constitutes a sustainable activity. I must investigate the structure of these Delegated Regulations. They are organized into Annexes. If the goal is the calculation of OpEx, I need to find the specific points within these Annexes that define the operational thresholds or the eligibility of operating costs.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis that the answer lies solely in Annex I. Annex I typically contains the criteria for the environmental objectives. But OpEx is not just about the objective; it is about the specific expenditure related to the activity. Looking deeper, I notice that the framework requires more than just a general reference to an Annex. There are specific subsections, such as the sub-points within Annex I, which appear to be the granular level where the operational technicalities are housed. If I only provide the Annex number, I am not providing the foundational reference for the calculation; I am providing the chapter.\n\nDoes Annex II play a role? In the structure of many EU environmental regulations, Annex II often pertains to different sets of criteria, such as the Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) requirements. If OpEx alignment depends on meeting both the substantial contribution and the DNSH criteria, then the references must span both Annexes. Furthermore, the calculation is not just a matter of reading the Annexes; it requires the authority of the Delegated Regulation itself. I must identify the specific regulatory instrument that carries these Annexes. \n\nI need to be careful not to conflate the disclosure requirement with the calculation rule. The KPI is the ratio of aligned OpEx to total OpEx. To determine the numerator, one must know if an expense is aligned. To know if it is aligned, one must apply the TSC. The TSC is found in the Annexes of the Delegated Regulation. Therefore, a complete solution must synthesize the disclosure mandate from the parent regulation with the specific technical definitions found within the Delegated Regulation's Annexes. The strongest direction involves identifying the interplay between the disclosure mandate in Article 8 and the specific technical points located within the granular subsections of both Annex I and Annex II of the relevant Delegated Regulation.",
    "text": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are Qwen, created by Alibaba Cloud. You are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nWhich regulatory references are foundational to the calculation of OpEx KPIs under the EU Taxonomy framework? <|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<|im_start|>think\nThe objective is to pinpoint the exact regulatory anchors used to compute Operating Expenditure (OpEx) Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) within the EU Taxonomy framework. A correct solution must distinguish between the high-level mandate for disclosure and the granular technical criteria that allow a practitioner to classify a specific expenditure as aligned. Ambiguity arises in the term foundational; does it refer to the primary Regulation that establishes the existence of the KPIs, or the Delegated Acts that provide the mathematical and qualitative thresholds for those KPIs? To solve this, I must distinguish between the primary actor, the European Commission, the mechanism of disclosure, and the technical outputs required for reporting.\n\nInitially, one might look at the Taxonomy Regulation (EU) 2020/852 itself. Article 8 of this regulation is the core mechanism for non-financial undertaking disclosure. It mandates that companies report their alignment regarding turnover, CapEx, and OpEx. However, Article 8 is a procedural rule—it tells you that you must report, but it does not provide the technical screening criteria required to determine if a specific Euro spent on maintenance, repairs, or administration counts as taxonomy-aligned. Therefore, citing only Article 8 is an incomplete path because it addresses the obligation of the actor rather than the specific calculation methodology needed for the output.\n\nI should shift focus to the Delegated Acts, specifically those defining the technical screening criteria (TSC). The calculation of OpEx KPIs is intrinsically tied to whether an activity meets the TSC. The framework is not just the parent Regulation but the specific Commission Delegated Regulations. These regulations contain the actual definitions of what constitutes a sustainable activity. I must investigate the structure of these Delegated Regulations. They are organized into Annexes. If the goal is the calculation of OpEx, I need to find the specific points within these Annexes that define the operational thresholds or the eligibility of operating costs.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis that the answer lies solely in Annex I. Annex I typically contains the criteria for the environmental objectives. But OpEx is not just about the objective; it is about the specific expenditure related to the activity. Looking deeper, I notice that the framework requires more than just a general reference to an Annex. There are specific subsections, such as the sub-points within Annex I, which appear to be the granular level where the operational technicalities are housed. If I only provide the Annex number, I am not providing the foundational reference for the calculation; I am providing the chapter.\n\nDoes Annex II play a role? In the structure of many EU environmental regulations, Annex II often pertains to different sets of criteria, such as the Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) requirements. If OpEx alignment depends on meeting both the substantial contribution and the DNSH criteria, then the references must span both Annexes. Furthermore, the calculation is not just a matter of reading the Annexes; it requires the authority of the Delegated Regulation itself. I must identify the specific regulatory instrument that carries these Annexes. \n\nI need to be careful not to conflate the disclosure requirement with the calculation rule. The KPI is the ratio of aligned OpEx to total OpEx. To determine the numerator, one must know if an expense is aligned. To know if it is aligned, one must apply the TSC. The TSC is found in the Annexes of the Delegated Regulation. Therefore, a complete solution must synthesize the disclosure mandate from the parent regulation with the specific technical definitions found within the Delegated Regulation's Annexes. The strongest direction involves identifying the interplay between the disclosure mandate in Article 8 and the specific technical points located within the granular subsections of both Annex I and Annex II of the relevant Delegated Regulation.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nAnnex I Point 1.1.3.1, Annex II, Del. Reg. C&P, Taxonomy Regulation Article 8, subsection 1.1.3.2 of Annex I<|im_end|>"
