sure. I suspect Article 8 is a key player here because it specifically addresses the disclosures required by financial market participants. This identifies a specific actor—financial market participants—and a specific mechanism—disclosure. But what about the actual technical content of that reporting? The regulation itself cannot contain every specific metric for every industry. This leads me to consider the role of Delegated Acts. The Disclosures Delegated Act is likely the procedural mechanism that translates the high-level requirements of the regulation into the actual templates and granular data points required for reporting. This looks like a much stronger direction for the compliance aspect.\n\nNow I need to address the compliance side of the equation—how an activity is actually judged to be compliant. This requires technical standards. I might initially think that the taxonomy creates its own entirely new standards from scratch, but that would be inefficient. It is more likely that it references existing EU directives to ensure consistency. For example, if an activity involves the energy efficiency of buildings, the taxonomy cannot exist in a vacuum; it must rely on the standards already established for building performance. This points toward Directive 2010/31/EU. If the taxonomy uses building energy performance as a benchmark for compliance, then that directive is a foundational legal provision for taxonomy-aligned activities in the real estate sector.\n\nI am wondering if I am missing the legal authority for how these rules are updated. Taxonomy criteria are not static; they evolve as technology changes. There must be a provision that allows the Commission to amend the technical annexes without a full legislative overhaul. This brings me back to the Taxonomy Regulation itself. I should investigate Article 16. If Article 16 governs the amendment of the technical screening criteria, then it is the essential legal provision that enables the ongoing compliance framework by allowing the list of compliant activities to change.\n\nThere is a lingering doubt about the energy sector. Does the taxonomy rely on the data from energy markets to verify compliance for energy-producing activities? If a company claims its energy production is green, the taxonomy needs a way to ensure that the underlying market data is reliable. I should look for a regulation that deals with market transparency. Regulation (EU) 2017/1369, which concerns the transparency of energy markets, could be the mechanism that provides the necessary data integrity for taxonomy compliance in the energy sector. This creates a link between market transparency and the ability to report accurately under the taxonomy.\n\nTo verify this direction, I will categorize the findings. The objectives are taxonomy alignment and transparency. The actors are financial market participants and companies. The mechanisms are the specific articles within the core regulation and the secondary delegated acts. The procedures are the technical screening and the actual disclosure processes. The outputs are the compliance reports. The strongest solution direction involves linking the primary Regulation (EU) 2020/852, specifically through the functions of Article 8 for disclosure mandates and Article 16 for the evolution of criteria, to the procedural requirements of the Disclosures Delegated Act, the technical baselines found in Directive 2010/31/EU, and the data transparency provided by Regulation (EU) 2017/1369. This multi-layered approach covers the legal mandate, the procedural implementation, the technical standards, and the data integrity required for the entire system to function.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nArticle 16 of Regulation (EU) 2020/852, Article 8 of EU Taxonomy Regulation, Directive 2010/31/EU, Disclosures Delegated Act, Regulation (EU) 2017/1369<|im_end|>"
