s to assume that R&D costs are categorized as operating expenses, or OpEx. If we know the total R&D spend and the proportion of employees working on non-contributory projects, we can attempt to bifurcate the OpEx. But wait, R&D is rarely purely OpEx; a significant portion of R&D is capitalized as CapEx. If I only look at OpEx, I miss the entire investment component of the taxonomy alignment. So, the calculation must involve both. This brings me to the concept of Full-Time Equivalents, or FTEs, as a proxy. Since it is notoriously difficult to track every single euro spent on a specific research project due to the overlapping nature of scientific inquiry, companies use FTEs as a granular, auditable proxy for resource allocation. If I can determine that a certain proportion of R&D employees are focused on non-contributory solutions, I can use that proportion to allocate the total R&D expenditure—both CapEx and OpEx—into aligned and non-aligned buckets. Let me test this. Suppose a company has 100 R&D employees. If 36 of them are working on projects that do not meet the technical screening criteria for climate mitigation, then 36% of the R&D budget is effectively non-contributory. This 36% would be excluded from the numerator when calculating the company's total taxonomy-aligned CapEx or OpEx. This would mean the company's eligibility percentage decreases. But is it possible that the proportion of employees doesn't perfectly map to the proportion of spend? Yes, because some employees might be more expensive than others, or certain projects might require more equipment and materials than others. However, from a regulatory reporting standpoint, using FTEs provides a defensible, standardized method to bridge the gap between qualitative project descriptions and quantitative financial figures. I must distinguish between the actors here: the R&D department provides the data, the finance department performs the allocation, and the regulator uses the resulting percentage to assess the company's green credentials. The mechanism is the conversion of labor hours into a financial weighting factor. The output is the final percentage of taxonomy-aligned spending. I initially thought the importance was just about avoiding greenwashing, but the real importance is the mathematical necessity of the FTE metric to solve the problem of cost attribution in complex, integrated R&D environments. Without identifying the non-contributory proportion, a company cannot accurately subtract the \"brown\" or \"neutral\" portions of its R&D investment from its total spending to arrive at a compliant alignment figure. The direction of the solution must focus on how the FTE-based allocation serves as the primary methodological bridge between headcount-level project descriptions and the high-level CapEx/OpEx percentages required by taxonomy reporting standards.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nApproximately 36% of employees in the Research & Development department, measured in full-time equivalents, focus on developing solutions that do not substantially contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.<|im_end|>"
