ical screening criteria. In the taxonomy framework, an activity can be eligible but not aligned. If an activity is eligible, the turnover is still classified under that objective, even if it is subsequently labeled as non-aligned because it failed to meet the specific environmental thresholds. If the report shows that no turnover was classified under the objectives at all, then it cannot be a case of being eligible but not aligned. If they were merely non-aligned, the turnover would still appear in the classification as being part of an eligible activity. This distinction between eligibility and alignment is crucial.\n\nThis leads me to a third possibility: the activities of the company are not covered by the taxonomy for these specific objectives. I need to differentiate between the actor, which is the company, and the mechanism, which is the classification of economic activities. If the company's primary economic activities—for instance, providing legal services or software development—do not have established technical screening criteria for the six environmental objectives, then those activities are not even considered eligible. If an activity is not eligible, it is not classified under the objectives in the first place. \n\nLet me test this. Suppose a company provides administrative consulting. Is administrative consulting an activity covered by the EU Taxonomy's environmental objectives? While consulting can support green transitions, the activity of \"administrative consulting\" itself is not an eligible economic activity under the current technical definitions. Because the activity is not in the scope of the regulation, the turnover generated from it cannot be classified as contributing to any of the six objectives. It is not that the consulting is \"bad\" or \"not green enough\"; it is that the activity is outside the predefined boundaries of the taxonomy's assessment scope. This would be categorized as being not eligible. I must ensure I am not conflating the quality of the business with the regulatory definition of the business. Even a highly sustainable consulting firm would report zero turnover for these objectives if their specific economic activity code is not recognized as eligible under the framework. The distinction between an activity being ineligible due to its nature versus being non-aligned due to its performance is the key to resolving this. The fact that no turnover was classified suggests the problem lies at the eligibility stage rather than the alignment stage.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nNo turnover was classified as contributing to the six environmental objectives under A.1 because these activities were explicitly not eligible for these criteria (N/EL1).<|im_end|>"
