ented, non-zero percentage because it is a technical, environmental assessment of individual projects. In contrast, a failure to meet Minimum Safeguards acts as a gatekeeper for the entire reporting framework. If the company’s social or governance compliance is found wanting, the \"alignment\" status for all activities is revoked. This provides a much more robust explanation for why the overall figure is exactly 0.0% despite the existence of eligible and contributing activities. I need to ensure I am not confusing the mechanism of contribution with the mechanism of alignment. An activity can contribute to climate mitigation (passing the second gate) but still fail to be aligned if the company itself fails the social compliance gate (the fourth gate). The distinction between the actors involved—technical experts for TSC/DNSH versus legal and compliance officers for Minimum Safeguards—further supports this. The strongest direction for the solution is to investigate the company's ability to demonstrate compliance with these social and governance safeguards during the 2023 reporting period, as this is the only mechanism that explains a total zeroing out of the alignment percentage.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nThe overall taxonomy-aligned CapEx for 2023 was reported as 0.0% because Hapag-Lloyd was unable to demonstrate compliance with minimum safeguards for the reporting year.<|im_end|>"
