ing the tool with the craftsman. What if the identity is actually rooted in their social responsibility, specifically through tenant relations and customer satisfaction? This would align with the social pillar of ESG. But is customer satisfaction an element of identity, or is it merely a performance metric? It feels like a metric. For it to be part of an identity, there must be a broader corporate self-image that views social stability and user needs as a foundational principle rather than just a target to hit. This leads me to a much more promising direction: the relationship between corporate self-image and strategic evolution. If the company’s identity is defined by its ability to adapt, then its self-image is not that of a traditional landlord, but of a modern, resilient provider. This shift in self-image would necessitate a total overhaul of their corporate strategy. I can test this by looking at the concept of new requirements. If a company has a static, traditional identity, it will view new environmental regulations or changing demographic shifts as threats to be resisted. But if the identity is inherently future-oriented, these new requirements become the very building blocks for creating new business models. This creates a causal chain: the self-image dictates the strategy, the strategy mandates the creation of a business model of the future, and that model is implemented through new business models designed to meet both regulatory requirements and social goals like customer satisfaction. I must be careful not to simply list these as separate items, but to show how they are interconnected elements of a single identity-driven framework. The strength of this path lies in its ability to connect the internal driver—the self-image—to the external output—the future-oriented practice. I have identified that the most robust way to solve this is to investigate how the company's redefined self-image acts as the catalyst for a structural shift in their business model to meet the demands of a changing world.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nbusiness model of the future, corporate self-image, corporate strategy, customer satisfaction, new business models, new requirements<|im_end|>"
