t not satisfy the requirement of mapping specifically to the Sustainable Development Goals. If the company wanted to be explicit about its SDG contributions, it would likely require an additional, more specialized tool.\n\nIf the GRI index is not the primary mapping tool for the SDGs, I should look for a dedicated SDG index. Such an index would be a structured procedure designed to list the 17 goals and provide a direct reference to the company's specific contributions or performance metrics for each. This would be a distinct reporting element from the GRI index, serving a different objective: the former ensures compliance with international reporting standards, while the latter ensures transparency regarding goal-specific alignment. \n\nI should also consider whether the mapping is integrated into the narrative of the report. A company might use an integrated reporting approach where the SDGs are woven into the text. But if the question asks for reporting elements used to map contributions, it implies a level of structure that a simple narrative lacks. A structured mapping is most effectively achieved through indices. \n\nI now have a hypothesis involving a multi-layered structure: the Sustainability Focus Areas provide the thematic organization, the GRI content index provides the standardized disclosure, and a specific SDG index provides the direct, goal-based mapping. I need to verify if these are truly distinct elements. The focus areas organize the narrative, the GRI index organizes the standard-based data, and the SDG index organizes the goal-based alignment. These three components work together to provide a comprehensive view. The strongest direction for a solution is to identify this combination of thematic areas and specialized indices, ensuring they are presented as the specific tools Evonik employs to bridge its operational impact with the global goals.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nGRI content index, SDG index, Sustainability Focus Areas, p. 140, p. 153<|im_end|>"
