001, I must ask if this covers the full scope of sustainability-related management. Does sustainability only encompass the environmental and the social, or does it include the governance required to make those systems functional? This is where I encounter a point of doubt. If the group's management systems are siloed, they might lack the organizational stability to be truly sustainable. A company could have environmental protocols but fail to execute them due to poor quality control or inconsistent operational processes. This suggests that the quality management system, ISO 9001, which is often categorized separately, might actually be a foundational component of their sustainability-related management architecture. I need to test this hypothesis. In large industrial organizations, management systems are often integrated into an Integrated Management System or IMS. In an IMS, the quality, environmental, and health and safety standards are not treated as separate entities but as a unified mechanism. Without ISO 9001 to provide the procedural rigor, the continuous improvement cycles required by ISO 14001 and the risk management required by ISO 45001 might fail. Thus, the objective is not just to find green certifications, but to find the certifications that validate the integrated systems that ensure the group's long-term operational sustainability. I will now focus on verifying whether the Andritz Group utilizes this integrated approach through the specific trio of ISO 9001, ISO 14001, and ISO 45001, as these would collectively provide the governance, environmental, and social management structures required.",
