transition of existing assets to higher efficiency levels. A strategy that ignores the retrofit or renovation aspect would be strategically flawed in a regulatory environment that emphasizes carbon reduction in existing building stock.\n\nSo, the strategy must encompass both the what and the how. The what relates to the specific real estate assets, and the how relates to the project lifecycle. If I combine these, I am looking for a framework that applies to specific asset classes and applies to both the creation of new assets and the improvement of existing ones. I need to identify which specific asset classes are central to their business model. For a company like Warimpex, this would involve high-value commercial real estate. If I include categories like hotels and office buildings, I cover the main drivers of their revenue. The mechanism of the strategy would then be the application of minimum sustainability standards and guidelines to these specific types of projects. The output is the certified property.\n\nLet's stress-test this combined direction. If the strategy includes both new construction and renovation projects across hotels and office buildings, does it meet the requirement of being a strategy to meet certification standards? Yes, because certification bodies like LEED or BREEAM offer distinct pathways for both new construction and building renovation. By applying a set of guidelines to both, the company ensures all major development activities are aligned with their ESG goals. The strongest path forward is to define the projects by combining the specific asset categories with the two primary modes of development: building from scratch and improving what already exists.",
