ntment booking, repair reporting, and complaint handling. In the legal cycle, we have document management, agreement access, name changes, and permits. In the financial/relational cycle, we have utility bills and direct chat. This categorization allows me to see if every feature maps back to the original requirements. The maintenance features drive engagement through responsiveness and efficiency through automation. The legal features drive efficiency through digitization and engagement through ease of use. The financial features drive efficiency through streamlined payments and engagement through transparency. The strongest direction is clearly this integrated service-delivery model where features are not just isolated tools but are part of a structured workflow that connects the tenant's immediate needs to the landlord's operational systems. I must ensure that I am not just listing features but ensuring they are logically connected to both engagement and efficiency. For example, a repair report is not just a piece of paper; it is a confirmation of service that closes a loop, which is the essence of engagement. Similarly, a digital utility bill is not just a cost; it is a data point for resource management. This multi-layered approach covers the administrative, operational, and communicative needs of the tenant.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nbooking repair appointments, chat with the customer manager, complaints process, documents management, name changes, permits, repair reports, tenancy agreement, utility bills<|im_end|>"
