ignificant global efforts. This leads me to a third, more robust path: instead of filtering by geography or company size, I will filter by the mechanism of the initiative itself. I will look for specific technical keywords in sustainability documentation, such as water circularity, zero-liquid discharge, effluent treatment plant upgrades, or chemical recovery systems. I need to distinguish between the actor, which is the Lenzing management, the mechanism, which is the specific technology used, the procedure, which is the implementation of these technologies at a plant, and the output, which is the reduction in pollution. I should test this by checking if a site mentioned in a report is a specialized facility. For example, if a site like Grimsby is mentioned in the context of textile-related environmental improvements, it must be included. I must be careful not to assume that a site is part of an initiative just because it is a large factory. I will also consider if air pollution reduction counts. If a site implements advanced scrubbers to reduce emissions, that fits the criteria of pollution reduction. The strongest solution direction involves cross-referencing the list of all Lenzing production sites against the specific technical descriptions of resource circularity and effluent management found in their environmental and sustainability reports. This requires mapping specific locations to mentions of water recycling, chemical recovery, or emission-control upgrades to ensure that the link between the site and the initiative is direct and documented.",
