 If I simply list LEED, I have omitted the actual performance benchmark that defines the building's sustainability status. If I only say Platinum, the information is useless without the framework. The strongest approach requires linking the full name of the standard with its specific achievement level.\n\nI must also be careful not to conflate different types of sustainability. I see the International WELL Building Institute mentioned. My initial thought might be to group this with the environmental LEED certification, as both are major industry standards. However, a deeper look at the mechanisms reveals a distinction: LEED is primarily an environmental mechanism, whereas WELL focuses on human health and well-being. These are different objectives and different actors. I must treat them as distinct certifications.\n\nFor the WELL certification, the text provides a specific detail: Gold Core & Shell. I need to test whether \"Core & Shell\" is an essential part of the answer or just a descriptor. In the context of building certification, \"Core & Shell\" is a crucial distinction regarding the scope of the procedure. It indicates that the certification applies to the building's primary structure and systems rather than the interior tenant fit-outs. If I were to simply state \"WELL Gold,\" I would be providing an incomplete and technically inaccurate reflection of the sustainability standard applied to that specific building. The scope is as much a part of the certification as the grade itself.\n\nI should consider a counter-hypothesis: could the answer be a simplified list of acronyms, such as DGNB, LEED, and WELL? While this is efficient, it fails the requirement to reflect the \"standards\" of the buildings. An acronym is a label for a system, but the \"standard\" of a specific building is the specific threshold it has met. A building that is LEED Bronze reflects a different standard than one that is LEED Platinum. Therefore, the acronym-only path is a failure of precision.\n\nI also considered whether I should categorize the results by their objectives, such as \"Environmental Certifications\" and \"Health Certifications.\" However, the question asks \"which certifications,\" not \"how are the certifications categorized.\" Categorization would be an additional layer of analysis that does not directly answer the prompt. The goal is to identify the specific certifications mentioned.\n\nThe most robust solution direction is to extract the full, integrated identifiers for each instance found in the text. This means I must not settle for acronyms or single words, but instead capture the complete semantic cluster that defines each certification: the certifying body, the formal name of the standard, and the specific achievement level or scope. This ensures that the distinction between an actor, a mechanism, and a performance output is maintained, providing a precise answer that reflects the actual sustainability status of the office buildings.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nGerman Sustainable Building Council (DGNB), Gold certificate, International WELL Building Institute, Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design (LEED), Platinum standard, WELL Gold Core & Shell certificate<|im_end|>"
