be excluded. I have to check the specific version mentioned in the documentation for each site.\n\nSo, the most robust solution path is not to look for \"large sites\" or \"certified sites\" in isolation, but to perform a cumulative audit. I will first establish the global energy baseline. Then, I will scan the site-by-site environmental data, specifically looking for European production sites. For every site that is European, I will check its certification status for the 2018 version of ISO 50001. If it passes that check, I will add its specific energy consumption to a running total. I will continue this process until I have evaluated all European sites. Finally, I will compare this cumulative sum to the global baseline. If the sum is greater than fifty percent, the specific names of the sites in that running total constitute the answer. This method is the only one that simultaneously respects the geographical constraint, the specific certification constraint, and the quantitative threshold. I must ensure that I am summing the energy of the sites themselves, not just the energy of the European region, to ensure the \"over half\" condition is met by the specific certified sites requested.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nBonn, Chedde, Lavradio, Meitingen, Muir of Ord, Nowy Sacz, Raciborz, Wiesbaden<|im_end|>"
