fficiency is achieved by integrating production chains to utilize every carbon atom and every heat unit. This suggests that the products in question are likely chemical intermediates that are part of highly integrated, cascading production streams. For example, if a site uses a steam cracker, the resulting C4 stream must be processed into various components to ensure no part of the stream is wasted. This would include building blocks like butadiene or butene-1, which are essential intermediates that allow the site to convert a single feedstock into a wide array of high-value products with minimal loss. I must now test whether these intermediates are sufficient. While they satisfy the process-integration mechanism, they do not necessarily address the functional efficiency mechanism. This leads to a second possible path: identifying products that enhance resource efficiency in the end-user's application. A prime example would be additives that improve the efficiency of fuel. If a chemical like an oxygenate is produced at the site and used to enhance combustion, it directly contributes to the efficient use of fuel resources globally. Similarly, in the realm of polymers, specialized additives like plasticizers can improve the performance and durability of materials, allowing for thinner or more efficient plastic components, which reduces the total volume of raw material needed. This creates a tension in my reasoning: are the products defined by how they are made at Marl or how they are used by the world? A comprehensive solution must bridge this gap. The strongest direction is to identify a group of products that satisfy both the Verbund-driven efficiency of the site and the functional efficiency of the market. I will stress-test this by looking for substances that are both characteristic of an integrated C4 or specialized organic chemistry chain and have clear, documented roles in enabling resource efficiency through combustion optimization or material performance. If 