 necessary visibility that the future controls will then govern. In this view, the engagement phase, involving mapping and questionnaires, identifies the actors and the scope of the supply chain, while the developing controls, such as audits, KPIs, and the Code of Ethics, provide the standards and the enforcement mechanisms. I must be careful not to conflate the Code of Ethics with an engagement tool; the Code is a control that dictates the rules of engagement. Therefore, the current engagement is the \"what\" and \"who,\" representing the mapping and transparency, and the developing controls are the \"how,\" representing the audits and evaluation criteria. This creates a causal link: without the current mapping and questionnaire-based engagement, the company would have no basis upon which to apply the forthcoming audits or KPIs. The process is \"emerging\" because it is moving from a state of descriptive visibility—knowing who the suppliers are—to a state of prescriptive accountability—measuring how they behave against new standards. I have tested the idea of a simple sequence and rejected it for lacking the integrative quality implied by \"combination.\" I have also tested the idea of replacement and rejected it for contradicting the word \"combines.\" The strongest direction for the solution is to demonstrate how the current information-gathering and mapping mechanisms serve as the essential empirical foundation upon which the planned regulatory and enforcement controls are being built.\n**Final Answer**\n<|im_start|>answer\nVEP87 used ESG questionnaires to map key suppliers, prioritised certifications and transparency, and stated that its developing Code of Ethics would extend integrity, safety, and human-rights expectations to the supply chain. It planned stronger ESG audits, formal evaluation criteria, supplier KPIs, and whistleblowing. The process therefore had an engagement and mapping foundation, but its formal policy and grievance controls were not yet fully implemented.<|im_end|>"
